{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The mechanism is physically inverted: drier (less humid) air has LOWER atmospheric longwave emissivity and therefore emits LESS downwelling longwave radiation toward the surface. With less downwelling longwave offsetting surface emission, the surface would lose net energy FASTER and cool MORE, not slower. Thus reduced humidity cannot 'slow radiative cooling' via 'lowered longwave emissivity' — the sign is backwards. A slower nocturnal cooling rate in drier air is opposite to observed physics; the target's premise contradicts the mechanism it invokes.","counterexample":["On a clear calm night, a dry desert surface cools far more rapidly and reaches a much lower minimum temperature than a humid surface under the same conditions — lower atmospheric emissivity accelerates net longwave cooling, contradicting the claim that reduced humidity 'slows radiative cooling.'"],"premise":"Drier air has lower longwave emissivity, which causes faster (not slower) net radiative cooling at the surface because downwelling atmospheric longwave is reduced.","target":"9c4ba607fb80dbf822bcaddec27ca28dda8abc6f7acbc5f28a1ac452c58ce835"}]}